9101: \label{thm:presentation-route-five-interface-sharpness}
9102: For the CICM presentation-only package whose primary source is
9103: \path{submission_abstract.tex}, whose supplement is this file, and whose selected
9104: support directory is \path{review_bundle/}, the maximal claim certified by the
9105: present theorem chain is exactly the conjunction of the following five bounded
9106: interfaces:
9107: \begin{enumerate}
9108: \item \emph{Conditional publication safety.}  The publication claim is a
9109: finite-record certificate under the externally supplied record-gate soundness
9110: predicate \(\mathsf{RGS}_{S}(P,L,\Gamma)\), not a proof that the gate
9111: implementations satisfy their specifications.
9112: \item \emph{Schema-relative package exactness.}  The executable verifier and the
9113: current-package certificate identify the finite rows accepted by the declared
9114: schema and dispatch table; they do not check proof semantics, theorem adequacy,
9115: or mathematical truth of all prose in the package.
9116: \item \emph{Bounded source-interface import.}  The imported newmath discovery-gate
9117: material is the pinned path/commit/label/line-range/bounded-summary interface of
9118: Table~\ref{tab:source-interface-record}, strengthened only by the finite
9119: table/JSON/snapshot comparison where that comparison is invoked.  It is not an
9120: exact reproof, exact source-statement import, public-source semantic-completeness
9121: result, Lean rebuild, BEDC rebuild, or axiom-purity certificate.
9122: \item \emph{Manifest-bounded case evidence.}  The Rule110 and GroundCompiler
9123: finite-witness claims are historical manifest rows with reviewable snapshots,
9124: status records, and stated limitation boundaries.  They are not fresh dynamic
9125: artifact validations, evaluator-correctness proofs, or semantic-exhaustiveness
9126: theorems for the artifacts.
9127: \item \emph{Operational support-surface condition.}  Bundle-backed claims are
9128: submitted-support claims only if the exact \path{review_bundle/} directory with
9129: the final digest manifest is uploaded, or if a single archive-equivalent byte
9130: locator supplies the same file list, manifest identity, SHA-256 digest rows, and
9131: relative path semantics.  In the current source package, absent such an external
9132: receipt or archive-equivalent record, the same claims remain conditional
9133: exact-inclusion or matching-archive claims.
9134: \end{enumerate}
9135: Moreover, by the finite-record certificate calculus spine of
9136: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-record-certificate-calculus-spine}, these five interfaces
9137: form a sharp bad-record skeleton.  Let
9138: \(c^{+}\) be any stronger reading of a load-bearing presentation-route claim that
9139: adds content on a nonempty set \(J\) of the five axes above.  Then \(c^{+}\) is
9140: accepted by the same finite-record publication chain if and only if, for every
9141: axis in \(J\), the submitted support surface adds the corresponding positive
9142: coordinate and the assigned pass record:
9143: \begin{enumerate}
9144: \item gate identity, gate specification, source state or version, and an
9145: implementation-soundness pass row or command-run tuple for axis~1;
9146: \item a semantic proof-checking, theorem-adequacy, exact-proof, Lean/BEDC rebuild,
9147: or out-of-schema verifier coordinate for axis~2;
9148: \item exact source-statement text, formal-source rebuild, axiom-purity record,
9149: public-source equality log, or semantic source-validation coordinate for axis~3;
9150: \item evaluator contract, artifact-semantic pass row, or fresh evaluator command
9151: run tied by manifest row to the submitted Rule110/GroundCompiler artifact for
9152: axis~4; and
9153: \item exact upload receipt for \path{review_bundle/} and
9154: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}, or one archive-equivalent locator
9155: passing the file-list, digest, manifest-identity, and path-semantics tests, for
9156: axis~5.
9157: \end{enumerate}
9158: If at least one required positive coordinate is absent, then \(c^{+}\) has an
9159: inclusion-minimal finite bad subrecord supported on one unpaid axis.  Consequently
9160: no mixture of the existing source-interface rows, verifier rows, theorem-inventory
9161: rows, historical case snapshots, or local bundle paths can glue together into an
9162: operational compiler-correctness, semantic theorem-correctness, dynamic
9163: artifact-validation, or unconditional submitted-support theorem.  Conversely,
9164: after all coordinates in the displayed union are added and pass their assigned
9165: gates, the same finite-extension normal form accepts precisely that upgraded
9166: finite-record reading.
9167: \end{theorem}
9168: 
9169: \begin{proof}
9170: We prove both maximality and sharpness axis by axis, and then take the finite
9171: union.  For axis~1, Definition~\ref{def:record-gate-soundness-external-interface}
9172: and Proposition~\ref{prop:record-gate-soundness-external-interface-exactness} make
9173: record-gate soundness an external input/output predicate, while
9174: Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} constructs finite publication
9175: certificates only under that predicate.  Theorems~\ref{thm:external-interface-projection-no-free-upgrade}
9176: and~\ref{thm:current-package-extension-coordinate-rigidity} identify an
9177: implementation-soundness reading as a strict extension requiring gate identity,
9178: specification, source-state or version, and implementation-soundness pass or
9179: command-run coordinates.  Thus the conditional finite-record reading is maximal
9180: until those coordinates are supplied, and the positive direction is exactly the
9181: extension criterion of Theorem~\ref{thm:implementation-soundness-extension-criterion}
9182: together with Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}.
9183: 
9184: For axis~2, Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness} proves exactness only
9185: for the declared schema predicate and the executable record verifier's projection,
9186: and Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness} specializes that
9187: schema-relative result to the current package.  The theorem-inventory side is
9188: bounded by Theorems~\ref{thm:deterministic-theorem-environment-extractor-boundary},
9189: \ref{thm:extractor-domain-exactness-non-gluing}, and
9190: \ref{thm:theorem-inventory-proofchecker-obstruction}: they provide syntactic
9191: labelled-environment coverage in the declared domain, not proof semantics or
9192: mathematical correctness.  Hence any semantic or adequacy reading reads a
9193: coordinate absent from the accepted schema projection unless it is added as a new
9194: proof-checking, exact-proof, formal-rebuild, or theorem-adequacy coordinate.
9195: 
9196: The finite union is governed by
9197: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-record-certificate-calculus-spine}: each axis is a
9198: projection of the same certificate tuple, and every upgrade is a positive
9199: extension budget rather than an informal combination of support rows.
9200: 
9201: For axis~3, Lemma~\ref{lem:source-interface-extraction-correctness} fixes the
9202: manual source-interface fields, Theorem~\ref{thm:bounded-source-interface-maximality}
9203: proves maximality of those bounded fields even after the finite comparison record
9204: is used, and Theorem~\ref{thm:source-interface-semantic-completeness-obstruction}
9205: shows that semantic source completeness is not determined by that projection.
9206: Therefore exact source import requires exact source text and byte equality,
9207: public-source equality requires a public fetch-and-compare log, formal rebuilds
9208: require Lean/BEDC command records, and semantic source-theory claims require a
9209: semantic-validation record; none is supplied by the bounded line-range interface.
9210: 
9211: For axis~4, Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-witness-manifest-record-interface} identifies
9212: the complete manifest-bounded coordinate set for the finite-witness evidence, and
9213: Corollary~\ref{cor:artifact-semantic-validity-boundary} plus
9214: Audit record~\ref{audit:case-snapshot-non-rerun-boundary} isolate the missing
9215: dynamic evaluator, artifact-semantic, and fresh-rerun fields.  Thus the accepted
9216: case evidence is historical and manifest-bounded, while dynamic Rule110 or
9217: GroundCompiler validation is accepted only after the evaluator contract, semantic
9218: pass row, or command-run tuple is added.
9219: 
9220: For axis~5, Proposition~\ref{prop:primary-supplement-interface-map} maps primary
9221: claims to supplemental theorem, table, and manifest support; Theorem~\ref{thm:exact-inclusion-support-surface-realization}
9222: proves that this support is realized by exact inclusion of \path{review_bundle/}
9223: with matching final digests or by an archive-equivalent replacement satisfying the
9224: finite byte and path tests; and Corollary~\ref{cor:current-no-receipt-support-surface-non-escalation}
9225: records that the present source package has no upload receipt, DOI, or
9226: institutional archive identifier.  Consequently the current support-surface claim
9227: is conditional until one of those operational finite records is present.
9228: 
9229: Now let \(c^{+}\) add content on the nonempty set \(J\) of axes.  Each added
9230: content item is a strict stronger reading in the preorder of
9231: Definition~\ref{def:stronger-reading-preorder} exactly when its corresponding
9232: positive coordinate is not already one of the accepted finite fields just listed.
9233: If every positive coordinate for every axis in \(J\) is present and passes its
9234: assigned gate, Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form} constructs
9235: the upgraded finite certificate.  If some required coordinate is absent, choose an
9236: unpaid axis and then an inclusion-minimal missing finite field in the budget for
9237: that axis.  The finite-coordinate theorem,
9238: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}, shows that two review
9239: states can agree on all submitted coordinates and differ on that missing field;
9240: therefore the stronger reading is not determined by the current certificate.
9241: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton} turns the missing field
9242: into an inclusion-minimal finite bad subrecord.  Adding records on any other axis
9243: does not change that projection, so mixed-interface gluing is impossible unless
9244: all unpaid axes are paid.  This proves the sharp skeleton, the lower bound, and
9245: the converse finite-extension acceptance statement.
9246: \end{proof}
9247: 
9248: \begin{theorem}[Supplement-level presentation-route stronger-reading classifier]
9249: \label{thm:presentation-route-stronger-reading-classifier}
9250: For the present CICM presentation-only package, every attempted stronger reading
9251: of the four flagged interface families is classified by the five-axis skeleton of
9252: Theorem~\ref{thm:presentation-route-five-interface-sharpness}, and the classifier
9253: is exact in the following sense.
9254: \begin{enumerate}
9255: \item A stronger reading of the finite publication-safety interface, the
9256: certificate-calculus spine, publication-safety maximality, or the current-package
9257: publication-safety instance is accepted exactly when it adds an axis~1
9258: implementation-soundness coordinate for record gates and, if the reading mentions
9259: proof truth, artifacts, submitted support, or venue outcome, the corresponding
9260: axis~2, axis~4, or axis~5 coordinates.
9261: \item A stronger reading of the four finite scripts, the finite-script RGS
9262: instantiation, or the assigned-gate soundness audit boundary is accepted exactly
9263: when it adds either the named finite projection coordinate already proved in
9264: Theorem~\ref{thm:concrete-finite-script-implementation-soundness}, or a separate
9265: axis~1/axis~2 implementation-soundness coordinate containing the verifier
9266: specification, source state, command-run tuple, log path, and assigned pass row
9267: for the stronger implementation claim.
9268: \item A stronger reading of the current-package acceptance ledger, certificate
9269: exactness theorem, or row-frozen invariance corollary is accepted exactly when it
9270: adds the missing axis~2 schema or semantic-proof coordinate, or the extra row,
9271: fresh command-run, artifact-semantic, upload, archive, or venue coordinate read by
9272: that stronger claim.
9273: \item A stronger reading of the imported source interface, source-record-file
9274: consistency, bounded source-interface maximality, or source-interface semantic
9275: completeness obstruction is accepted exactly when it adds the missing axis~3
9276: coordinate: exact source-statement text, byte-equality or public-reachability log,
9277: formal Lean/BEDC rebuild record, axiom-purity record, or semantic source-validation
9278: record, together with the assigned pass row for that coordinate.
9279: \end{enumerate}
9280: If a proposed stronger reading in any of these four families lacks at least one
9281: coordinate listed above, then it has an inclusion-minimal finite bad subrecord on
9282: an unpaid axis.  Conversely, if all coordinates required by its listed axes are
9283: submitted and pass their assigned gates, the finite-record certificate calculus
9284: accepts precisely that upgraded finite-record reading.  Hence the theorem chain
9285: is maximal for the conditional, row-frozen, schema-relative, bounded-source,
9286: finite-projection claims stated in this paper and no mixed use of existing rows
9287: can silently upgrade them to implementation soundness, semantic truth, public
9288: source completeness, artifact validity, support upload, or venue acceptance.
9289: \end{theorem}
9290: 
9291: \begin{proof}
9292: Each of the four families is a load-bearing presentation-route claim and is
9293: therefore first projected to the seven-coordinate certificate tuple
9294: \((K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)\) by
9295: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-record-certificate-calculus-spine}.  A proposed stronger
9296: reading is strict precisely when it changes a coordinate not determined by that
9297: projection, by Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}.  The
9298: five-axis theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:presentation-route-five-interface-sharpness},
9299: then identifies the unique axis on which the changed coordinate lives and lists
9300: the positive finite coordinates that must be paid for acceptance.
9301: 
9302: For the first family, Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} assumes
9303: record-gate soundness and constructs only finite publication certificates;
9304: Proposition~\ref{prop:publication-safety-maximality} and
9305: Corollary~\ref{cor:publication-safety-current-package-instance} inherit exactly
9306: that conditional interface.  Therefore an implementation-soundness reading changes
9307: axis~1 by adding gate specification, source-state, command-run, and pass-row
9308: coordinates.  If the same reading also asserts proof truth, artifact semantics,
9309: support upload, or venue acceptance, those phrases change axes~2, 4, or~5 exactly
9310: as listed in Theorem~\ref{thm:presentation-route-five-interface-sharpness}.
9311: 
9312: For the second family,
9313: Theorem~\ref{thm:concrete-finite-script-implementation-soundness} proves only the
9314: printed finite predicate on the parsed finite projection of each named script;
9315: Corollary~\ref{cor:finite-script-rgs-instantiation-boundary} and
9316: Theorem~\ref{thm:assigned-gate-soundness-audit-boundary} use those rows only as
9317: finite command-run coordinates.  A claim about broad verifier correctness or gate
9318: semantics reads a verifier specification, source state, execution environment,
9319: runtime semantics, log, and assigned pass row not contained in the projection.
9320: Thus it is an axis~1 or axis~2 extension, while the already proved projection
9321: claim is exactly the finite coordinate supplied by the command-run record.
9322: 
9323: For the third family,
9324: Proposition~\ref{prop:current-package-acceptance-certificate-ledger},
9325: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness}, and
9326: Corollary~\ref{cor:row-frozen-current-package-invariance} read only the displayed
9327: six rows, their certificate fields, the submitted support surface, and the
9328: recorded verifier log.  A semantic truth certificate, a seven-row or modified-row
9329: state, a fresh execution, an artifact-validity statement, an upload or archive
9330: claim, or a venue decision changes a finite coordinate outside that six-row
9331: projection.  Those are respectively axis~2, axis~4, or axis~5 extension
9332: coordinates, or a row-set extension governed by the same budget normal form.
9333: 
9334: For the fourth family,
9335: Lemma~\ref{lem:source-interface-extraction-correctness},
9336: Proposition~\ref{prop:source-interface-record-file-consistency},
9337: Theorem~\ref{thm:bounded-source-interface-maximality}, and
9338: Theorem~\ref{thm:source-interface-semantic-completeness-obstruction} determine
9339: only the pinned path, commit, label, line range, extraction date, bounded summary,
9340: local JSON/table/snapshot comparison, and declared evidence level.  Exact
9341: source-statement import, public reachability, byte equality against a public
9342: checkout, formal rebuilds, axiom-purity checks, and semantic source-validation
9343: require new axis~3 coordinates and assigned pass rows.
9344: 
9345: If all coordinates required by the axes touched by the stronger reading are
9346: present and pass their assigned gates, the extension-budget normal form,
9347: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}, constructs the enlarged
9348: certificate and Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness} accepts it at the
9349: declared evidence level.  If any required coordinate is absent, choose one unpaid
9350: axis and one missing finite field on that axis.  The finite-coordinate theorem
9351: gives two review states agreeing on every submitted coordinate and differing on
9352: that missing field, so the stronger reading is not determined by the current
9353: certificate.  The obstruction skeleton,
9354: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton}, reduces the failure to
9355: an inclusion-minimal bad subrecord.  Since the five axes are projections of the
9356: same certificate tuple, adding rows on a different axis cannot alter the unpaid
9357: projection.  This proves exact classification, maximality, and the no-gluing
9358: boundary for all four flagged families.
9359: \end{proof}
9360: 
9361: \begin{theorem}[End-to-end upgrade decomposition obstruction]
9362: \label{thm:end-to-end-upgrade-decomposition-obstruction}
9363: For the present CICM presentation-only package, an end-to-end workflow-correctness
9364: reading of the publication-safety theorem chain is accepted by the finite-record
9365: calculus if and only if it decomposes into the finite positive-coordinate budgets
9366: of Theorem~\ref{thm:presentation-route-five-interface-sharpness} and every touched
9367: budget is supplied with its assigned pass record.  More explicitly, any reading
9368: asserting correctness of the actual gate implementations, fresh source or Lean
9369: rebuilds, semantic truth of the package claims, dynamic Rule110 or
9370: GroundCompiler artifact validity, current post-edit execution, uploaded
9371: supplemental support, or venue outcome touches respectively one or more of the
9372: gate-implementation, schema/proof, source-interface, artifact-semantic,
9373: command-boundary, support-surface, and venue coordinates listed in the
9374: five-interface skeleton.  If one of those coordinates is absent, the stronger
9375: end-to-end reading has an inclusion-minimal finite bad subrecord and no mixture
9376: of the existing finite source rows, pass rows, theorem-inventory rows, historical
9377: case snapshots, or local bundle paths can certify it.  Conversely, after exactly
9378: the missing coordinates and assigned pass records are added, the finite-extension
9379: normal form certifies only the corresponding upgraded finite-record reading, not
9380: any further semantic or venue fact.
9381: \end{theorem}
9382: 
9383: \begin{proof}
9384: Let \(e\) be an attempted end-to-end reading of the theorem chain.  Project \(e\)
9385: to the seven certificate fields \((K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)\) of
9386: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-record-certificate-calculus-spine}.  If \(e\) says no more
9387: than the finite publication certificates, row-frozen current-package exactness,
9388: bounded source import, manifest-bounded case evidence, and conditional support
9389: surface already listed in Theorem~\ref{thm:presentation-route-five-interface-sharpness},
9390: then it is the existing finite-record theorem chain.
9391: 
9392: Otherwise \(e\) is a strict stronger reading in the preorder of
9393: Definition~\ref{def:stronger-reading-preorder}.  The stronger-reading classifier,
9394: Theorem~\ref{thm:presentation-route-stronger-reading-classifier}, sends each
9395: changed assertion to one of the five axes of
9396: Theorem~\ref{thm:presentation-route-five-interface-sharpness}.  The concrete
9397: phrases in the statement are precisely those axes: gate implementation correctness
9398: uses axis~1; semantic proof truth, schema escape, and theorem adequacy use
9399: axis~2; fresh source or Lean/BEDC rebuild and source semantic completeness use
9400: axis~3; dynamic Rule110 or GroundCompiler artifact semantics use axis~4; uploaded
9401: supplemental support, archive replacement, and venue outcome use axis~5, with
9402: fresh post-edit execution also reading the command-boundary coordinate required by
9403: the relevant axis.  Hence the end-to-end reading decomposes into the finite union
9404: of the touched positive-coordinate budgets.
9405: 
9406: If every coordinate in that union is present and every assigned gate row passes,
9407: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form} constructs the enlarged
9408: certificate, and Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness} accepts it exactly
9409: at the declared evidence level.  This proves the positive direction, including
9410: the final caveat: a finite extension certifies only the finite fields it adds.
9411: 
9412: If some coordinate in the union is absent, choose an unpaid touched axis and an
9413: inclusion-minimal missing field on that axis.  By
9414: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}, two review states may
9415: agree on all submitted coordinates and differ on that missing field, so the
9416: end-to-end assertion is not determined by the current package.  The obstruction
9417: skeleton, Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton}, identifies
9418: the missing field as an inclusion-minimal finite bad subrecord.  Because the five
9419: axes are projections of the same certificate tuple, adding or reusing rows on a
9420: different axis leaves this unpaid projection unchanged.  Thus existing finite
9421: source rows, pass rows, theorem-inventory rows, historical case snapshots, and
9422: local bundle paths cannot glue into end-to-end workflow correctness.  This proves
9423: the obstruction and the converse lower bound.
9424: \end{proof}
9425: 
9426: \begin{corollary}[Presentation-summary non-escalation]
9427: \label{cor:presentation-summary-non-escalation}
9428: Let \(s\) be a load-bearing sentence in the primary presentation source
9429: \path{submission_abstract.tex} or in an accompanying presentation abstract that
9430: summarizes Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface},
9431: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness},
9432: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-command-result-closure},
9433: Theorem~\ref{thm:presentation-route-stronger-reading-classifier}, or
9434: Theorem~\ref{thm:presentation-route-five-interface-sharpness}.  If \(s\) is
9435: accepted by the same finite-record publication chain without adding new support
9436: coordinates, then its strongest accepted normal form is the following bounded
9437: reading: under the externally supplied record-gate-soundness predicate, the
9438: visible finite records determine the schema-relative certificate rows and their
9439: finite bad-subrecord obstructions.  In particular, the accepted presentation
9440: summary is finite-record only and does not assert gate implementation soundness,
9441: end-to-end compiler correctness, proof-assistant correctness, dynamic artifact
9442: validity, upload or archive availability, venue compliance, or venue acceptance.
9443: Conversely, a presentation summary that explicitly keeps the phrases
9444: ``under record-gate soundness'' and ``finite-record only'' for this theorem chain,
9445: and that makes no additional implementation, semantic, artifact, upload, archive,
9446: or venue claim, is accepted at the same finite-record level as the corresponding
9447: certificate rows.
9448: \end{corollary}
9449: 
9450: \begin{proof}
9451: Treat the summary sentence \(s\) as a presentation-route claim whose evidence
9452: surface is the primary source and the supporting theorem chain in this
9453: supplement.  Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} constructs only the
9454: certificate tuple \((K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)\) from the submitted finite surface, ledger,
9455: dispatch table, and record-gate-soundness input.  Its conclusion already excludes
9456: implementation soundness, proof-assistant correctness, artifact semantics,
9457: daemon behavior, upload, archive availability, venue compliance, and venue
9458: acceptance unless the corresponding finite extension coordinates are added.
9459: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness} and
9460: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-command-result-closure} specialize this same tuple to
9461: the current schema rows and refreshed command-result rows, so they add no new
9462: semantic or operational coordinate.
9463: 
9464: Theorem~\ref{thm:presentation-route-five-interface-sharpness} classifies the five
9465: presentation-route axes and proves the exact extension budget for every stronger
9466: reading on any nonempty set of those axes.  Therefore a summary sentence accepted
9467: without additional coordinates can project only to the conditional
9468: record-gate-sound, finite-record certificate reading.  If the sentence is read as
9469: gate implementation soundness, end-to-end compiler correctness, proof-assistant
9470: correctness, dynamic artifact validity, upload or archive availability, venue
9471: compliance, or venue acceptance, it has added content on at least one of the five
9472: axes.  The five-interface theorem then supplies an inclusion-minimal missing
9473: coordinate as a finite bad subrecord unless that coordinate and its assigned pass
9474: row have been supplied.  This proves the non-escalation part.
9475: 
9476: For the converse, the displayed bounded wording asserts exactly the positive
9477: coordinates already present in the publication-safety certificate and current
9478: package rows: the record-gate-soundness premise, the finite support surface, the
9479: schema-relative verifier projection, and the finite bad-subrecord boundary.  It
9480: does not add any coordinate from the five extension budgets.  The schema-relative
9481: exactness theorem and the five-interface sharpness theorem therefore accept the
9482: summary at precisely the same finite-record level as the underlying certificate
9483: rows.
9484: \end{proof}
9485: 
9486: \begin{theorem}[Finite-record support-coordinate fixed-point normal form]
9487: \label{thm:submission-support-fixed-point-normal-form}
9488: For the current source package, the support state for the three externalized
9489: claims in the publication-safety theorem chain is exactly the following finite
9490: normal form.  This is a bookkeeping theorem for the machine-checkable support
9491: coordinates used by the bounded audit interface: it records when byte strings,
9492: logs, and digest rows are mutually current, and it is not an independent
9493: semantic invariant of the paper, the artifacts, or the venue process.
9494: \begin{enumerate}
9495: \item The theorem-inventory coordinate is accepted only after
9496: \path{theorem_inventory.json} and \path{theorem_inventory.md} have been written,
9497: the theorem-environment extractor and finite-record verifiers have been rerun on
9498: that post-inventory state, and
9499: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md} has been regenerated over those
9500: bytes and logs.
9501: \item Bundle-backed support is local/path-verified only unless the exact
9502: \path{review_bundle/} directory is uploaded with matching final digests or an
9503: archive-equivalent locator satisfying
9504: Audit record~\ref{audit:review-bundle-archive-equivalence} is supplied.
9505: \item Venue/page-limit compliance is only a dated local-readiness observation
9506: unless a near-submission upload-time venue record, or an included unchanged-rule
9507: statement tying the upload to the latest dated venue record, is supplied.
9508: \end{enumerate}
9509: Consequently no theorem-ledger, bundle-availability, or upload-time venue claim
9510: may be read in the stronger submitted-support sense unless the corresponding
9511: finite coordinate in this normal form is present.  The only mathematical role of
9512: this theorem in the article is to close the support-coordinate input required by
9513: Theorems~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} and
9514: \ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness}; later corollaries in this
9515: subsection are projections of this same finite byte-level closure.
9516: \end{theorem}
9517: 
9518: \begin{proof}
9519: For the theorem-inventory coordinate, the digest table is a function of finite
9520: bytes.  If \path{theorem_inventory.json} or \path{theorem_inventory.md} is
9521: created or changed after that table is computed, its byte string is not an input
9522: to the recorded hash row.  The fixed-point closure theorem,
9523: Theorem~\ref{thm:final-support-surface-closure}, the post-inventory certificate
9524: Proposition~\ref{prop:post-inventory-fixed-point-support-certificate}, and the
9525: realization theorem
9526: Theorem~\ref{thm:post-inventory-finite-record-realization}, therefore accept the
9527: inventory coordinate exactly after the inventory files and their verifier logs
9528: precede the final digest pass.  This proves the first clause and prevents stale
9529: digest rows from supporting the exact-inclusion theorem-ledger claim.
9530: 
9531: For bundle-backed support,
9532: Proposition~\ref{prop:submission-support-instantiation-field-normal-form}
9533: records no current upload receipt and records the archive-equivalent locator only
9534: as a digest-conditioned public branch/path coordinate.  By
9535: Corollary~\ref{cor:current-no-receipt-support-surface-non-escalation} and
9536: Proposition~\ref{audit:review-bundle-availability-condition}, a local path under
9537: \path{review_bundle/} is therefore not an external reviewer-available coordinate
9538: unless the exact-upload record is present or the public coordinate contains the
9539: matching digest-identified bytes.  This proves the second clause.
9540: 
9541: For venue compliance, the venue facts are external facts rather than internal
9542: source definitions.  Theorem~\ref{thm:venue-rule-freshness-non-escalation} and
9543: Corollary~\ref{cor:upload-time-venue-compliance-record-requirement} classify the
9544: current venue logs as dated readiness records and require either a later
9545: upload-time tuple or an unchanged-rule statement before the stronger upload-time
9546: claim is available.  This proves the third clause.  The three fields are
9547: independent coordinates of the finite package state, so adding a stronger
9548: reading in any one field without its named coordinate is exactly the prohibited
9549: coordinate extension ruled out by
9550: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}.  Hence the displayed
9551: normal form is exact.
9552: \end{proof}
9553: 
9554: \begin{theorem}[Finite-record post-inventory support-coordinate dichotomy]
9555: \label{thm:post-inventory-support-coordinate-dichotomy}
9556: For the current presentation package there are exactly two possible support
9557: readings for any claim whose evidence surface names \path{theorem_inventory.json},
9558: \path{theorem_inventory.md}, or a file under \path{review_bundle/}.  If the
9559: ordered post-inventory pass of Theorem~\ref{thm:post-inventory-package-fixed-point}
9560: has been run and the exact resulting surface is externally instantiated by the
9561: upload-or-archive record of
9562: Corollary~\ref{cor:upload-or-archive-instantiation-record}, then the claim is a
9563: submitted bundle-backed support claim at the finite-record evidence level stated
9564: in its certificate.  In every other case it is only a local/path-verified or
9565: dated-readiness claim, with the further fallback of
9566: Theorem~\ref{thm:submission-support-fixed-point-normal-form} if the
9567: post-inventory pass is not closed.  No intermediate reading is available in
9568: which refreshed local digests alone supply an external support coordinate, or in
9569: which an upload-or-archive locator alone supplies post-inventory verifier and
9570: digest closure.
9571: \end{theorem}
9572: 
9573: \begin{proof}
9574: Let $c$ be a claim whose evidence surface names one of the stated paths.  The
9575: finite-coordinate determination boundary,
9576: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}, allows $c$ to be
9577: read only from finite fields present in the current package state.  The first
9578: field is the post-inventory closure field.  By
9579: Theorem~\ref{thm:post-inventory-package-fixed-point}, that field is present
9580: exactly when the inventory files are written, the theorem-environment extractor
9581: and finite-record verifiers are rerun on that post-inventory state, any retained
9582: dated route-readiness log is fixed, and the final SHA--256 manifest is generated
9583: after those bytes exist.  If this field is absent, the first clause of
9584: Theorem~\ref{thm:submission-support-fixed-point-normal-form} permits only the
9585: local index or dated-readiness fallback and forbids the exact theorem-ledger
9586: support reading.
9587: 
9588: The second field is external instantiation of the selected support surface.  By
9589: Corollary~\ref{cor:upload-or-archive-instantiation-record} and
9590: Proposition~\ref{audit:review-bundle-availability-condition}, this field is
9591: present only when either the exact supplemental directory with the matching final
9592: digest manifest is uploaded, or an archive-equivalent replacement satisfying
9593: Audit record~\ref{audit:review-bundle-archive-equivalence} is supplied.  If this
9594: field is absent, Corollary~\ref{cor:current-no-receipt-support-surface-non-escalation}
9595: keeps every bundle-backed support claim local/path-verified and prevents it from
9596: becoming an unconditional public or venue-submitted support coordinate.
9597: 
9598: Thus the support state is the product of two independent finite fields: local
9599: post-inventory closure and external support instantiation.  When both are
9600: present, the submitted bundle-backed reading follows from the exact support
9601: surface closure theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:final-support-surface-closure}.  If the
9602: first field is missing, the verifier/digest fixed point is not closed; if the
9603: second is missing, the closed local surface has not been externally instantiated.
9604: In either missing-field case, promoting $c$ to a submitted bundle-backed claim
9605: would add a finite coordinate not contained in the package.  Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}
9606: rejects that promotion, and the only remaining readings are precisely the
9607: local/path-verified or dated-readiness readings already classified above.
9608: \end{proof}
9609: 
9610: \begin{proposition}[Final refreshed-coordinate instantiation]
9611: \label{prop:final-refreshed-coordinate-instantiation}
9612: For the current dated local-readiness package, the first coordinate of
9613: Theorem~\ref{thm:submission-support-fixed-point-normal-form} is instantiated by
9614: the finite tuple consisting of \path{theorem_inventory.json},
9615: \path{theorem_inventory.md}, \path{review_bundle/theorem_environment_extraction_run.log},
9616: \path{review_bundle/certificate_verification_run.log},
9617: \path{review_bundle/source_interface_verification_run.log},
9618: \path{review_bundle/primary_claim_inventory_freshness_2026-06-09.log},
9619: \path{review_bundle/VENUE_BIBLIOGRAPHY_LIVE_CHECK_2026-06-09.log}, and
9620: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}, provided these files are submitted
9621: with the exact SHA-256 rows printed in the digest manifest.  This instantiation
9622: does not add an upload receipt, DOI, venue acceptance record, fresh Lean rebuild,
9623: daemon run, or artifact rerun; the archive-equivalent locator remains the public
9624: branch/path coordinate and is effective only with matching digest rows.
9625: \end{proposition}
9626: 
9627: \begin{proof}
9628: The tuple is finite, and each named component is one of the coordinates already
9629: classified by Proposition~\ref{prop:post-inventory-fixed-point-support-certificate},
9630: Theorem~\ref{thm:post-inventory-finite-record-realization},
9631: Proposition~\ref{audit:command-run-boundary-inventory}, and
9632: Theorem~\ref{thm:submission-support-fixed-point-normal-form}.  The two theorem
9633: inventory files are the post-inventory byte coordinates.  The theorem-environment
9634: log is the syntactic coverage coordinate for those files.  The two finite-record
9635: verifier logs and the primary-claim verifier log are the schema-relative,
9636: source-interface, and primary-surface coordinates.  The
9637: venue/bibliography log is the dated external-route locator coordinate.  The
9638: digest manifest is the finite hash table selecting the bytes of the preceding
9639: coordinates.
9640: 
9641: If the submitted files have the printed hashes, then equality of SHA-256 rows is
9642: exactly the package-state equality relation used by
9643: Theorem~\ref{thm:workspace-final-package-reconciliation}; hence the first normal
9644: form coordinate is present.  If any named file is omitted, changed after the
9645: digest pass, or replaced by a file with a different row, then the finite tuple no
9646: longer equals the selected coordinate and the post-inventory claim falls back to
9647: the weaker local or historical reading described in
9648: Proposition~\ref{prop:regenerated-theorem-inventory-digest-closure}.  The final
9649: sentence follows from Corollary~\ref{cor:current-no-receipt-support-surface-non-escalation}
9650: and Corollary~\ref{cor:upload-time-venue-compliance-record-requirement}: none of
9651: the listed files is an upload receipt, DOI, venue acceptance, Lean rebuild,
9652: daemon execution, or artifact-rerun record; the branch/path locator is already
9653: classified separately by the support-surface normal form.
9654: \end{proof}
9655: 
9656: \begin{theorem}[Finite-record post-inventory support-coordinate fixed point]
9657: \label{thm:post-inventory-package-fixed-point}
9658: Let $P$ be the current local package state consisting of the manuscript source,
9659: \path{submission_abstract.tex}, \path{theorem_inventory.json},
9660: \path{theorem_inventory.md}, and \path{review_bundle/}.  Suppose that the following
9661: finite sequence is executed in the displayed order:
9662: \begin{enumerate}
9663: \item write the theorem-inventory files for the manuscript source state;
9664: \item rerun \path{python review_bundle/extract_theorem_environments.py};
9665: \item rerun the finite-record verifiers named in
9666: Table~\ref{tab:command-boundary-inventory};
9667: \item rerun the dated venue/bibliography locator check, if a dated route-readiness
9668: claim is retained; and
9669: \item write \path{review_bundle/final_digest_generation_run.log} as the command
9670: metadata for the hash pass and regenerate
9671: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md} from the resulting bytes of the
9672: inventory files, verifier logs, dated venue log, and selected review-bundle files
9673: other than the digest manifest and this generation log.
9674: \end{enumerate}
9675: Then, at the same bookkeeping level as
9676: Theorem~\ref{thm:submission-support-fixed-point-normal-form}, the support coordinate selected by
9677: Proposition~\ref{prop:final-refreshed-coordinate-instantiation} is a fixed point
9678: of the finite post-inventory verifier/digest pass: every file used to justify the
9679: theorem-ledger, finite-verifier, and dated-readiness claims is an input to the
9680: final digest manifest, the digest-generation command has its own acyclic command,
9681: source-state, environment, exit-code, and log-path row in
9682: Table~\ref{tab:command-boundary-inventory}, and the manifest is produced after
9683: those support inputs exist.
9684: Conversely, if any one of the five ordered steps is omitted or performed after the
9685: digest regeneration, the stronger exact-inclusion theorem-ledger claim is not
9686: available; the manuscript retains only the local path-verified or dated-readiness
9687: reading classified in
9688: Theorem~\ref{thm:submission-support-fixed-point-normal-form}.  In either case the
9689: fixed point supplies no external upload receipt, archive-equivalent locator,
9690: upload-time venue-compliance record, fresh Lean/BEDC rebuild, marker-resolution
9691: audit, axiom-purity audit, daemon run, or artifact-semantic rerun.
9692: \end{theorem}
9693: 
9694: \begin{proof}
9695: The package state $P$ is finite because it is a finite list of relative paths and
9696: finite byte strings.  The extractor in the second step reads
9697: \path{main.tex} and \path{theorem_inventory.json}; its stated boundary is
9698: syntactic theorem-like environment extraction and inventory-label coverage only.
9699: The finite-record verifiers in the third step read the schema, pass-record,
9700: primary-claim, submission-interface, source-interface, manifest, and source